r/value "y=1.4-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375051} {:answer/id #uuid "c01cc395-1e94-5e8f-af96-f3e97f4de178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2+1.4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375052} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6b41dd-5085-565c-bcc4-f1f87a7e36c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2+1.4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375053} {:answer/id #uuid "801ce760-7e23-5f9a-ad15-99c8abf027b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2-1.4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c01cc395-1e94-5e8f-af96-f3e97f4de178" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2+1.4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375052} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6fdd51a-e92c-5013-bf78-c8f49f8d0960" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f59e05eb-a82f-5ff7-9613-77ece9768e07" :question/math-academy-id "q-139617" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1.4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=2+1.4x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "950c1ed7-f804-54f5-bf97-16bf2d1a663b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa48db7d-42b9-542f-9132-5f9480ba31ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-1.7 - 6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130744} {:answer/id #uuid "b293a154-6bc4-55d9-96c8-478e48edb3f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 6 - 1.7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130745} {:answer/id #uuid "0a91356e-c0af-5bcb-8392-8c31bb66251a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-1.7 + 6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130746} {:answer/id #uuid "2d1d51eb-c2c0-594e-8a70-852787541bb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-6 + 1.7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130747} {:answer/id #uuid "54ac3134-61ee-573c-99be-ef16ece83c67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 1.7 - 6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b293a154-6bc4-55d9-96c8-478e48edb3f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 6 - 1.7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130745} :answer-field/id #uuid "00e530e9-f1e8-5bc9-9ded-9ed90868c02d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72cc6e31-5631-5692-b09e-242241827364" :question/math-academy-id "q-139618" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $6$ | $4$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1.7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=6-1.7x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "950c1ed7-f804-54f5-bf97-16bf2d1a663b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "318b84e7-b565-5c43-b7fe-ffdbc38a9d4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3+7.5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375057} {:answer/id #uuid "78927ed4-3c4f-502d-9e21-7127daaa25d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3+7.5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375058} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3df94b-3d58-5d5d-8d32-afccf8202945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=7.5+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375059} {:answer/id #uuid "851b72e0-f836-5dbd-9bb8-057a49cde8c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3-7.5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375060} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce6b563-d759-500f-af33-7e8ef97768a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=7.5-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b3df94b-3d58-5d5d-8d32-afccf8202945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=7.5+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375059} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e717ba2-d538-5eac-b9b2-cc2ec7556eae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe413604-3b29-5884-9f6d-636ad3b77fd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-139643" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $5$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation \n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7.5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=7.5+3x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "950c1ed7-f804-54f5-bf97-16bf2d1a663b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be591578-ea10-5e90-a071-4312dfbfea80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6.25+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375064} {:answer/id #uuid "d74c8c82-c7de-585e-9fe2-797eeb2dbebd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2-6.25x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375065} {:answer/id #uuid "f36030fa-a7fe-5317-98ee-1ddeac603aab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6.25-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375066} {:answer/id #uuid "1630c3fe-314c-5989-aaea-7c97ec9d0fed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2-6.25x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375067} {:answer/id #uuid "98431726-886b-53c6-8135-8d832d173775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2+6.25x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be591578-ea10-5e90-a071-4312dfbfea80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6.25+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375064} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8201e7d-a5ed-59d2-8542-b4f6ff6c5f03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a1d4b018-0144-59c5-a052-81600bc8633a" :question/math-academy-id "q-139646" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $5$ | $7$ | $12$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{56}[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{56}[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6.25 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=6.25+2x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "628717b8-4d7c-5201-a50c-a2a14f9d925c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$4\\,400$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375071} {:answer/id #uuid "6c4e3e0a-6fa6-54d1-8c13-a844ab31c878" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$4\\,200$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375072} {:answer/id #uuid "b7efd5a7-9470-5ec3-aeb2-a3ccabeee676" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3\\,700$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375073} {:answer/id #uuid "85e28c32-b691-5661-bf19-15b081857652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3\\,200$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375074} {:answer/id #uuid "5e00fa0e-9e68-5fe8-a248-f64702a9d47d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$2\\,800$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375075}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85e28c32-b691-5661-bf19-15b081857652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$3\\,200$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375074} :answer-field/id #uuid "98bcbbb1-f12c-5d23-9b4c-213292e8d0cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d0607109-09b8-52c8-8857-0db36b038506" :question/math-academy-id "q-139735" :question/problem "A company collected data on the sale price (in thousands of dollars) of a particular used car model and the car's age (in years). This data is shown below.\n\n| Age $(x)$ | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$6$** | **$7$** | **$8$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Price $(y)$ | $12$ | $10$ | $8.5$ | $7$ | $6.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the sale price of a model that is $10$ years old, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}38 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}44 \\\\ 250\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 8.5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6.5\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}38 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}44 \\\\ 250\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17.2 \\\\ -1.4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=17.2-1.4x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the selling price of a car of the same model that is $10$ years old, we substitute $x=10$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =17.2-1.4(10) \\\\  & =3.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the sale price will be $$3\\,200.$\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=10$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "777ff542-a657-5249-be0a-93a1da42bf20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$826$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375078} {:answer/id #uuid "fc036480-0155-5a05-af43-6f4aaf86f5da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$872$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375079} {:answer/id #uuid "2dbe9689-b2b8-5838-b210-1b670233ec1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$902$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375080} {:answer/id #uuid "3f0c1fdf-8390-59e7-8b89-99f515b96225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$868$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375081} {:answer/id #uuid "8554e9db-06be-5c81-8521-c98dea6b7335" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$906$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8554e9db-06be-5c81-8521-c98dea6b7335" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$906$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375082} :answer-field/id #uuid "10471fd5-efae-5858-aec5-7c6b12872d9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a7db993-94b0-573c-8bae-99041a111b86" :question/math-academy-id "q-139736" :question/problem "A statistician collected data on the height above the ground (in hundreds of meters) of a hot air balloon during the first five minutes of its flight. This data is shown below. \n\n| Time $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Height $(y)$ | $1.6$ | $3.0$ | $4.6$ | $6$ | $7.6$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the balloon's height after $6$ minutes, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}22.8 \\\\ 83.4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1.6 \\\\ 3.0 \\\\ 4.6 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7.6\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}22.8 \\\\ 83.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.06 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.06+1.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the height of the balloon after $6$ minutes, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.06+1.5(6) \\\\  & =9.06\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the balloon's height after $6$ minutes will be $906$ meters.  \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375083 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43af6b1f-c10c-5d7b-a095-813f288cee75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.75$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375085} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d4ed2b-77d1-5f16-999e-6217a5829b22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.15$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375086} {:answer/id #uuid "1d4aa237-3168-52c3-b900-5d4cc90dfa52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.25$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375087} {:answer/id #uuid "2135a0e6-7b97-5fff-bdd7-b1adf6e3127a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.25$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375088} {:answer/id #uuid "b40d0f96-3fdd-508a-84c1-73757c94203f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.75$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43af6b1f-c10c-5d7b-a095-813f288cee75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.75$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375085} :answer-field/id #uuid "07dda353-44b6-532b-a9e9-8b95d1a31f93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3ba26ac4-59dd-55c3-b644-94e5b96251c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-139749" :question/problem "A bungee jumping instructor measures how much a bungee rope stretches (in meters) when one end of the rope is attached to the bridge railing while a weight of $x$ kilograms is hung on the other. This data is shown below.\n\n| Weight $(x)$ | **$10$** | **$20$** | **$30$** | **$40$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Stretch $(y)$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, how much will the rope stretch if the instructor uses a $65$-kilogram mass, assuming the trend continues?  \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that* \n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{500}[\\begin{aligned}750 & -25 \\\\ -25 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 200\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 20 \\\\ 1 & 30 \\\\ 1 & 40\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1.5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2.5\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{500}[\\begin{aligned}750 & -25 \\\\ -25 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 200\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 0.05\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.5+0.05x.$\n\nFinally, to predict how much the rope will stretch if a $65$-kilogram mass is hung from it, we substitute $x=65$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5+0.05(65) \\\\  & =3.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the rope will stretch $3.75$ meters if a $65$-kilogram mass is hung from it.\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=65$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "979c5af4-9964-538b-b144-532063ca3f7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130762} {:answer/id #uuid "d9d790f1-d20b-5c8a-bbc1-23c707806914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130763} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf336cd-ac26-5998-9be3-a638605f8e3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8.5$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130764} {:answer/id #uuid "64aa2687-8d50-568b-9155-b17f093ad1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9.5$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130765} {:answer/id #uuid "7d17029a-45a5-5838-89f1-19c40f3f3fb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.5$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9d790f1-d20b-5c8a-bbc1-23c707806914" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$9$ feet" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130763} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb67d2ca-15d7-5506-b1d1-9f75b40c28f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8f426df0-1f38-5a2e-b701-5e427a0357ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-139750" :question/problem "A biologist has recorded data on a pine tree's age (in years) and height (in feet). This data is shown below. \n\n| Age $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Height $(y)$ | $1.2$ | $2.5$ | $3.6$ | $4.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the tree's height after $8$ years, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1.2 \\\\ 2.5 \\\\ 3.6 \\\\ 4.5\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 1.1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.2+1.1x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the  height after $8$ years, we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.2+1.1(8) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, according to the model, the height of a pine tree that is $8$ years old will be $9$ feet. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=8$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line in Context"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37c5db0c-e66d-5543-ae71-95d8e42cae2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$29.8$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375092} {:answer/id #uuid "7b2c4175-0dad-5828-81f5-51eae679a5d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$28$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375093} {:answer/id #uuid "4fdcf1f5-3f8c-59f2-8141-b1670b3417ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$28.5$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375094} {:answer/id #uuid "13c21312-ef78-5636-9ef4-eb03d19fb588" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$27.9$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375095} {:answer/id #uuid "abef495e-2d84-5a1b-a72c-830ee2b2725b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$29$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37c5db0c-e66d-5543-ae71-95d8e42cae2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$29.8$ million" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375092} :answer-field/id #uuid "a062b0c2-57df-58cd-b63e-ffcb26cc9864" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e04b9559-7385-5bf2-a965-e72e3bc7ef64" :question/math-academy-id "q-41467" :question/problem "The relationship between the advertising budget and sales (both in millions of dollars) of a company per year over several years is shown in the following table. \n\n| Advertising $(x)$ | **$2$** | **$2.5$** | **$3$** | **$3.5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Sales $(y)$ | $10$ | $12$ | $16$ | $20$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the total sales when the annual advertising budget is equal to $$5$ million, assuming the trend continues? \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}31.5 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}58 \\\\ 168\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2.5 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3.5\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}31.5 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}58 \\\\ 168\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4.2 \\\\ 6.8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-4.2+6.8x.$ \n\nFinally, to predict the value of the sales when the advertising budget is equal to $$5$ million, we substitute $x=5$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4.2+6.8(5) \\\\  & =29.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the total sales when the advertising budget equals $$5$ million will be $$29.8$ million. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=5$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "950c1ed7-f804-54f5-bf97-16bf2d1a663b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Least-Squares Regression Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b50f6cc-5982-5757-b3f5-35019865b293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2.5+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375099} {:answer/id #uuid "0d60aee2-1192-5b5f-88ba-01cb66a78821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3-2.5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375100} {:answer/id #uuid "dfff1481-c209-56ab-81cd-e541eaaa6f0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2.5-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375101} {:answer/id #uuid "55f45631-e7c2-5f2f-abcd-13fcad804ca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2.5+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375102} {:answer/id #uuid "dc095137-668e-561b-93f7-f8e886d1e2a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3+2.5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d60aee2-1192-5b5f-88ba-01cb66a78821" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3-2.5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375100} :answer-field/id #uuid "4763825e-7183-5fd5-b7f9-6831662cbeee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375098}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fe0bbef-9859-5f74-ae5f-623d0f639f9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-41738" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $3$ | $2$ | $-3$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector. \n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=3-2.5x.$"}]}
